{"variant": "U2_expl_fmt", "id": "ode_second_exponential_positive_root", "repeat": 0, "tier": "medium", "field": "probability-ode", "language": "en", "outcome": "fallthrough", "reason": "explanation_rejected", "kind": "prove", "answer": null, "tactic": "model_0", "tactic_text": "constructor\n· simp\n· intro x\n  simp [Real.deriv_exp]", "verified": true, "formal": "(fun t : ℝ => Real.exp t) 0 = 1 ∧ ∀ x : ℝ, deriv (deriv (fun t : ℝ => Real.exp t)) x - (fun t : ℝ => Real.exp t) x = 0", "proved": "(fun t : ℝ => Real.exp t) 0 = 1 ∧ ∀ x : ℝ, deriv (deriv (fun t : ℝ => Real.exp t)) x - (fun t : ℝ => Real.exp t) x = 0", "explanation": "Let $f:\u001b[0m\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be given by $f(t)=\\exp(t)$. We verify its value at $0$ and its differential equation using the derivative rule for the exponential.\n\nAt $t=0$, the exponential has value $\\exp(0)=1$, so $f(0)=1$.\n\nFor any $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$, the derivative rule gives $f'(x)=\\exp(x)$; applying the same rule again gives $f''(x)=\\exp(x)$. Since $f(x)=\\exp(x)$, subtracting yields $f''(x)-f(x)=0$. This holds for every real $x$.\n\nThus $f(0)=1$ and $f''(x)-f(x)=0$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.", "request": "The function f(t)=exp t has f(0)=1 and satisfies f''(x)-f(x)=0 for every real x.", "seconds": {"first_call": 2.604, "closed": 2.905, "verified": 3.368, "gate": 4.74, "explained": 7.844, "explanation_review": 2.397, "reviewed": 10.242, "total": 10.242}, "explanation_approved": false, "explanation_issue": "rejected:The learner-visible overview contains a malformed control character in the type annotation (`$f:\u001b[0m\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$`), so the mathematical expression is not properly formatted as KaTeX-compat", "item_usd": 0.000834, "statements": ["(fun t : ℝ => Real.exp t) 0 = 1 ∧ ∀ x : ℝ, deriv (deriv (fun t : ℝ => Real.exp t)) x - (fun t : ℝ => Real.exp t) x = 0"], "gate": {"passed": true, "reasons": [], "agree": true, "reviewed": true, "roundtrip": true, "issue": "", "rt_issue": ""}, "calls": [{"lab