{"variant": "U2_expl_fmt", "id": "trig_expand_tan_square_plus_one", "repeat": 0, "tier": "hard", "field": "trigonometry", "language": "en", "outcome": "fallthrough", "reason": "explanation_rejected", "kind": "prove", "answer": null, "tactic": "model_0", "tactic_text": "intro x hx\nrw [Real.tan_eq_sin_div_cos]\nfield_simp\nnlinarith [Real.sin_sq_add_cos_sq x]", "verified": true, "formal": "∀ x : ℝ, Real.cos x ≠ 0 → Real.tan x ^ 2 + 1 = 1 / (Real.cos x) ^ 2", "proved": "∀ x : ℝ, Real.cos x ≠ 0 → Real.tan x ^ 2 + 1 = 1 / (Real.cos x) ^ 2", "explanation": "Fix a real number $x$ with $\\cos(x) \\neq 0$. The proof rewrites tangent using sine and cosine, then uses the Pythagorean identity.\n\nBy the definition of tangent, $\\tan(x)=\\sin(x)/\\cos(x)$. Since $\\cos(x)\\neq 0$, we may clear the denominators in the claimed equality, reducing it to an equality involving only powers of $\\sin(x)$ and $\\cos(x)$.\n\nThe identity $\\sin^2(x)+\\cos^2(x)=1$ supplies that equality: multiplying it by the appropriate powers of the nonzero quantity $\\cos(x)$ gives exactly the cleared-denominator relation. Dividing back by those nonzero powers yields the desired formula.\n\nTherefore, for every real $x$ with $\\cos(x)\\neq 0$, $\\tan^2(x)+1=1/\\cos^2(x)$.", "request": "For every real x, if cos(x) is not equal to 0, then tan(x)^2 + 1 = 1/cos(x)^2.", "seconds": {"first_call": 2.746, "closed": 4.44, "verified": 5.238, "gate": 5.849, "explained": 7.707, "explanation_review": 2.333, "reviewed": 10.04, "total": 10.04}, "explanation_approved": false, "explanation_issue": "rejected:The explanation does not state the cleared-denominator equality or specify the powers used. It therefore leaves the essential algebraic step from the Pythagorean identity to the claimed formula unsupp", "item_usd": 0.000935, "statements": ["∀ x : ℝ, Real.cos x ≠ 0 → Real.tan x ^ 2 + 1 = 1 / Real.cos x ^ 2", "∀ x : ℝ, Real.cos x ≠ 0 → Real.tan x ^ 2 + 1 = 1 / (Real.cos x) ^ 2"], "gate": {"passed": true, "reasons": []